# 14. Modular Inverse — Turning Division Into Multiplication

After all the previous steps, our problem has been transformed into something much simpler.

Originally we had:

```text
512-bit Numerator

÷

Even Denominator
```

After removing the remainder, factoring out powers of two, and shifting the upper bits into the lower bits, we now have:

```text
256-bit Numerator

÷

Odd Denominator
```

This is exactly the form required for the modular inverse.

---

# Where We Are

At this point,

our code is:

```solidity
uint256 inverse = (3 * denominator) ^ 2;
```

followed by

```solidity
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
inverse *= 2 - denominator * inverse;
```

and finally

```solidity
result = lowerPart * inverse;
```

Everything we did previously was preparing for these lines.

---

# The Big Question

Why don't we simply write

```solidity
lowerPart / denominator;
```

The answer is:

Because the numerator originally came from a

```text
512-bit number.
```

The EVM has no instruction for

```text
512-bit ÷ 256-bit
```

division.

However,

the EVM is extremely good at

```text
256-bit multiplication.
```

So instead of dividing,

OpenZeppelin transforms the problem into a multiplication.

---

# Normal Mathematics

In ordinary mathematics,

division can always be rewritten.

Example

```text
100 ÷ 5
```

is exactly the same as

```text
100 × (1 / 5)
```

Both equal

```text
20.
```

Notice what happened.

The division disappeared.

It became a multiplication.

---

# Why Can't Solidity Do This?

Suppose

```text
denominator = 5.
```

Then

```text
1 / 5

=

0.2
```

But Solidity only has integers.

It cannot store

```text
0.2.
```

So writing

```solidity
lowerPart * (1 / denominator)
```

is impossible.

The value

```text
1 / denominator
```

does not exist as an integer.

---

# The Modular Inverse

Instead,

we compute a special integer called the

```text
Modular Inverse.
```

This integer satisfies

```text
denominator × inverse

≡

1

(mod 2²⁵⁶)
```

Read this slowly.

If we multiply

```text
denominator
```

by

```text
inverse,
```

we do **not** obtain

```text
1
```

in ordinary arithmetic.

We obtain

```text
1
```

on the giant

```text
2²⁵⁶-position clock.
```

That is all modular arithmetic means.

---

# Child Analogy

Imagine a giant clock with

```text
2²⁵⁶
```

numbers.

Instead of asking

```text
"What is 1 ÷ denominator?"
```

we ask

> **"Which number brings us back to 1 after multiplying on this giant clock?"**

That number is called

```text
inverse.
```

---

# Why Is It Called An Inverse?

Many people ask:

> "Why call it an inverse? Why not just call it a number?"

Because its entire purpose is to **undo multiplication**.

Suppose

```text
denominator = 5.
```

If

```text
5 × inverse ≡ 1
```

then multiplying by

```text
inverse
```

has the same effect as dividing by

```text
5.
```

It is literally the multiplicative inverse of

```text
5
```

inside modulo

```text
2²⁵⁶.
```

That is why the mathematical name is

```text
Modular Multiplicative Inverse.
```

---

# Why Must The Denominator Be Odd?

Earlier we removed every factor of

```text
2.
```

This was not an optimization.

It was a mathematical requirement.

A modular inverse modulo

```text
2²⁵⁶
```

exists only if

```text
gcd(denominator, 2²⁵⁶) = 1.
```

Since

```text
2²⁵⁶
```

contains only factors of

```text
2,
```

the denominator must contain **no factor of two.**

In other words,

it must be

```text
Odd.
```

That is exactly why we removed all powers of two in the previous section.

---

# The Journey So Far

Originally

```text
512-bit Numerator

↓

Remove remainder

↓

Exact division

↓

Remove powers of two

↓

Odd denominator

↓

Shift upper bits into lower bits

↓

256-bit Numerator

÷

Odd Denominator
```

Now,

for the first time,

the modular inverse can be computed.

Everything before this point was simply preparing the problem for this mathematical trick.

---

# Key Takeaways

* The EVM cannot directly perform a 512-bit ÷ 256-bit division.

* Ordinary division can be rewritten as multiplication by a reciprocal.

* Solidity cannot represent fractional reciprocals such as `1/5`.

* Instead, OpenZeppelin computes a **modular inverse**, an integer satisfying:

  ```
  denominator × inverse ≡ 1 (mod 2²⁵⁶)
  ```

* Multiplying by this modular inverse has the same effect as dividing by the denominator within modulo `2²⁵⁶`.

* The denominator must be odd; otherwise, a modular inverse does not exist.

---

# Flow So Far

```text
512-bit Product
        ↓
Remove Remainder
        ↓
Exact Division
        ↓
Remove Powers Of Two
        ↓
Denominator Becomes Odd
        ↓
Shift Upper Bits Into LowerPart
        ↓
256-bit Numerator
        ↓
Compute Modular Inverse
        ↓
Replace Division With Multiplication
```
